1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,843
Many believe that the story first began in America in 1877,

2
00:00:04,843 --> 00:00:08,740
when two friends were arguing over

3
00:00:08,740 --> 00:00:14,530
whether a horse ever had all four feet or hooves off the ground when it galloped.

4
00:00:14,530 --> 00:00:19,139
To settle the bet, a photographer was asked to photograph a horse galloping

5
00:00:19,139 --> 00:00:21,920
and the bet was settled

6
00:00:21,920 --> 00:00:26,940
because you could see that all the hooves were off the ground in some of the photos.

7
00:00:26,940 --> 00:00:33,460
What was even more interesting was that if the photos were shown in quick succession

8
00:00:33,840 --> 00:00:38,930
the horse looked like it was running-in other words 'moving pictures'.

9
00:00:38,930 --> 00:00:48,600
The person who became interested in taking the moving pictures to its next step was the famous American inventor Thomas Edison.

10
00:00:48,600 --> 00:00:51,330
Actually, he didn't do the work himself

11
00:00:51,330 --> 00:00:57,570
but rather asked a young Scotsman in his employ to design a system, which he did.

12
00:00:57,570 --> 00:01:00,380
Now this young fellow was clever

13
00:01:00,380 --> 00:01:07,406
because the first thing he did was study other systems-primitive as they were-of moving pictures

14
00:01:07,406 --> 00:01:14,900
and then put all the existing technologies together to make the first entire motion picture system.

15
00:01:14,900 --> 00:01:19,500
He designed a camera, a projection device and the film.

16
00:01:19,500 --> 00:01:26,400
The system was first shown in New York in 1894 and was really very popular.

17
00:01:26,400 --> 00:01:31,410
Apparently people lined up around the block to see the wonderful new invention.

18
00:01:31,410 --> 00:01:35,640
There were, however, a couple of problems with the system.

19
00:01:35,640 --> 00:01:39,100
The camera weighed over 200 kilograms

20
00:01:39,120 --> 00:01:42,850
and only one person at a time could see the film.

21
00:01:42,850 --> 00:01:47,503
Well now, news of the new system in America travelled fast

22
00:01:47,503 --> 00:01:54,270
and a number of rival European systems started to appear once people had heard about it.

23
00:01:54,270 --> 00:02:01,477
The single problem with all the systems was they couldn't really project the film onto a screen. You know,

24
00:02:01,477 --> 00:02:04,680
so more than one person could see it.

25
00:02:04,680 --> 00:02:09,880
Then in 1895 three systems were all developed

26
00:02:09,880 --> 00:02:13,500
more or less at the same time and independently of each other.

27
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:18,748
I guess the most famous of these was by the Lumiere Brothers from France,

28
00:02:18,748 --> 00:02:22,160
and they called their system the cinematograph

29
00:02:22,520 --> 00:02:25,120
which of course is where the word cinema comes from.

30
00:02:25,120 --> 00:02:29,720
There were also two brothers in Germany who developed a successful system

31
00:02:29,780 --> 00:02:31,580
and they called it a bioskop.

32
00:02:31,580 --> 00:02:35,496
Well now, once the problem of projection had been solved,

33
00:02:35,496 --> 00:02:41,370
the next challenge for the inventors was to make the films longer and more interesting.

34
00:02:41,370 --> 00:02:46,640
A continuing problem at the time was that the films had a tendency to break

35
00:02:46,640 --> 00:02:48,981
when they were being played-a problem

36
00:02:48,981 --> 00:02:52,500
which was caused by the tension between the two wheels

37
00:02:52,880 --> 00:02:56,230
or 'reels' as they are called, which hold the film.

38
00:02:56,230 --> 00:03:00,870
Now this problem was solved by two American brothers.

39
00:03:00,870 --> 00:03:03,460
They developed the 'Lantham Loop

40
00:03:03,840 --> 00:03:07,816
which was the simple addition of a third reel between the two main reels,

41
00:03:07,816 --> 00:03:13,300
and this took all the tension away with the result that the film stopped snapping.

42
00:03:13,300 --> 00:03:19,786
So now there was a real possibility of having films of more than two or three minutes,

43
00:03:19,786 --> 00:03:25,890
and this led to the making of The Great Train Robbery - the very first movie made.

44
00:03:25,890 --> 00:03:31,040
It only lasted 11 minutes but was an absolute sensation

45
00:03:31,040 --> 00:03:33,472
and there were cases of people watching the movie

46
00:03:33,472 --> 00:03:38,260
and actually fainting when the character fired a gun at the camera!

47
00:03:38,260 --> 00:03:41,032
Almost overnight movies became a craze,

48
00:03:41,032 --> 00:03:50,270
and by 1905 people in America were lining up to see movies in 'store theatres', as they were called then.

49
00:03:50,270 --> 00:03:58,939
I guess the next big step in terms of development of technology was to have people actually talking on the film,

50
00:03:58,939 --> 00:04:03,340
and the first step towards this was in 1926

51
00:04:03,580 --> 00:04:06,370
when sound effects were first used on a film.

52
00:04:06,370 --> 00:04:11,560
It wasn't until the following year however that the first 'talkie

53
00:04:11,600 --> 00:04:13,560
as they were called then, was made.

54
00:04:13,560 --> 00:04:18,180
This film featured actors speaking only during parts of the film

55
00:04:18,180 --> 00:04:20,660
and was called The Jazz Singer,

56
00:04:20,660 --> 00:04:25,671
and it wasn't until 1928 that the first all-talking film was produced,

57
00:04:25,671 --> 00:04:29,430
and this was called The Lights of New York.

58
00:04:29,430 --> 00:04:33,871
Unfortunately, the sound on this early film was not very good

59
00:04:33,871 --> 00:04:37,907
and I believe they put subtitles on the film. That is,

60
00:04:37,907 --> 00:04:44,770
they printed the dialogue along the bottom of the film to compensate for this poor sound quality.

61
00:04:44,770 --> 00:04:47,960
Now, with the addition of sound

62
00:04:48,340 --> 00:04:51,450
moving pictures became far more difficult to make...
